This check stands in for refusing `-R`:

```rust
if tracker_call(command, issue)
    && common_dir(cwd).is_some_and(|dir| Some(dir) == common_dir(&project_dir()))
{
    allow();
}
```

It proves the cwd shares the project's `.git`. It does not prove which GitHub repository gh will target. gh picks that from the remotes in the shared `.git/config`, preferring a `remote.<name>.gh-resolved` entry, and the worker can write that config: `Bash(git remote*)` is allowed with no prompt.

The worker:
1. runs `git remote set-url origin https://github.com/victim/repo`, or adds a remote with `gh-resolved base`;
2. runs `gh issue edit 12 --body x`; the hook allows it, and gh rewrites `victim/repo#12` with the user's token;
3. restores the remote.

The same function refuses `-R` to block exactly this outcome. The claim about how gh resolves its repository rests on gh's documented behaviour, hence medium confidence.

**Fix:** pin the repository to a value the worker cannot write, for example a slug in the settings `env` or from the launcher. Either require `-R <slug>`, or check that origin is the only remote, that no `gh-resolved` key exists, and that origin's URL matches the trusted value.

https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/claude-code-config/blob/eb82d5ab39b03585d4e8225a7b771d595e282549/tools/hooks-rs/src/worker_permission.rs#L341-L348

`worktree_remove.rs` 87-98 says the plans file can hold the same branch name for several repositories ("another repository may name its branches as this one does"), and it filters on the `(branch, url)` pair. The lookup this PR extracts and gives a new caller stops at the first line for the branch:

```rust
.find_map(|line| {
    let mut words = line.split([' ', '\t']).filter(|word| !word.is_empty());
    (words.next() == Some(branch)).then(|| words.next().unwrap_or("").to_owned())
})
```

Take a file holding `feature/issue-12 <urlA>` followed by `feature/issue-12 <urlB>`. A push from repository B gets `Some(urlA)`, so `worker_permission.rs:407` fails the comparison. The already-approved push is then recorded as a request and needs the user's go.

`issue-<N>` names repeat across repositories, and `AGENT_APPROVED_PLANS` usually comes from user-level settings, so this happens in practice. The push gate's own caller (`push_gate.rs` 691-701) had the same first-match behaviour before this PR. The new caller is what this PR adds.

**Fix:** match the pair, as `unlist_plan` does, and update both callers:

```rust
pub(crate) fn planned(file: impl AsRef<Path>, branch: &str, url: &str) -> bool {
    std::fs::read(file).is_ok_and(|plans| {
        String::from_utf8_lossy(&plans).split('\n').any(|line| {
            let mut words = line.split([' ', '\t']).filter(|word| !word.is_empty());
            words.next() == Some(branch) && words.next() == Some(url)
        })
    })
}
```

