No test pins the guard that keeps non-Bash tools out of the allow rules. The mutant that survives is `let command = input["command"].as_str().unwrap_or_default();`:
- The `Tracker` and `Plan` fixtures hard-code `"tool_name": "Bash"` (`worker_permission_tracker.rs:24`, `worker_permission_push.rs:39`).
- `Lead::ask_as` is the only route to another tool, and no test passes it a `command` key.

A test along these lines, in `tests/worker_permission_tracker.rs`, catches it. Adjust the names to the file's own fixtures:

```rust
#[test]
fn a_tracker_command_in_a_tool_that_is_not_bash_takes_the_grant_route() {
    let tracker = Tracker::new("wpt-not-bash");
    let input = json!({"command": COMMENT});
    let verdict = tracker.lead.ask_as(WORKER, &tracker.repo, "mcp__x__run", input);
    assert_eq!(verdict.behavior, "deny");
    assert!(!verdict.id().is_empty(), "{}", verdict.message);
}
```

https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/claude-code-config/blob/eb82d5ab39b03585d4e8225a7b771d595e282549/tools/hooks-rs/src/worker_permission.rs#L333-L336

The test harness `hook()` calls `env_clear()` (`tests/common/mod.rs:39-47`), so `AGENT_WORKER` is only ever unset or set to a name, never set to an empty string. If someone drops the empty-name filter, the suite still passes, but a session that exports `AGENT_WORKER=` would have every call denied. The hook is meant to sit in settings that every session reads, so that is a real input.

```rust
#[test]
fn an_empty_agent_worker_gets_no_decision() {
    let payload = json!({"tool_name": "Bash", "tool_input": {"command": "ls"}}).to_string();
    let mut command = hook("worker-permission");
    command.env("AGENT_WORKER", "");
    let out = run(&mut command, &payload);
    assert_eq!((out.code, out.stdout.as_str()), (0, ""));
}
```

